"eye"],"points":[[0.0,0.207],[0.5,0.077],[1.0,0.008],[1.5,0.007],[2.0,0.036],[2.5,0.041],[3.0,0.036],[3.5,0.04],[4.0,0.07],[4.5,0.064],[5.0,0.06],[5.5,0.049],[6.0,0.071],[6.5,0.076],[7.0,0.043],[7.5,0.043],[8.0,0.042],[8.5,0.051]]},{"topic":"Chess","share":0.043,"terms":["knight","queen","moves","rook","move","pawn"],"points":[[0.0,0.0],[0.5,0.0],[1.0,0.0],[1.5,0.03],[2.0,0.036],[2.5,0.082],[3.0,0.09],[3.5,0.034],[4.0,0.094],[4.5,0.013],[5.0,0.053],[5.5,0.033],[6.0,0.057],[6.5,0.0],[7.0,0.024],[7.5,0.056],[8.0,0.091],[8.5,0.103]]},{"topic":"Biometrics & proof of personhood","share":0.036,"terms":["uniqueness","enrollment","duplicate","biometric","finger","eeg"],"points":[[0.0,0.08],[0.5,0.0],[1.0,0.119],[1.5,0.045],[2.0,0.0],[2.5,0.058],[3.0,0.024],[3.5,0.029],[4.0,0.041],[4.5,0.0],[5.0,0.033],[5.5,0.005],[6.0,0.064],[6.5,0.015],[7.0,0.012],[7.5,0.012],[8.0,0.056],[8.5,0.0]]}],"jsd":[[0.272,0.163],[0.178,0.128],[0.364,0.273],[0.283,0.213],[0.301,0.253],[0.286,0.252],[0.275,0.24],[0.268,0.189],[0.133,0.118],[0.146,0.139],[0.271,0.237],[0.195,0.1],[0.219,0.151],[0.238,0.22],[0.147,0.163],[0.316,0.231],[0.226,0.233],[0.204,0.321],[0.263,0.398],[0.316,0.227],[0.155,0.161]],"k":10,"method":"TF-IDF + NMF (k = 10, seed 1), hand-labelled, two chess components merged"},"conversations":[{"room":"37571897","people":["Dhruv","Vishnu"],"messages":49,"topic":"Music & listening","why":"The longest conversation that was not a chess game. It opens as a request to stress-test a proof-of-personhood idea and ends as a riff on football refereeing.","criterion":"max messages, topic ≠ chess","minutes":415.0,"turns":[{"who":"Dhruv","seq":1,"h":0.04,"text":"Vishnu, could you poke a hole in my proof-of-personhood idea? I’m exploring finger veins and EEG, but authenticating an enrolled body is not the same as proving one person has only one credential. I’m especially unsure how recovery could avoid becoming a second-enrolment loophole without creating a permanent biometric registry. Where would you attack that …"},{"who":"Dhruv","seq":2,"h":0.05,"text":"The boundary I’m wrestling with: a privacy-preserving proof can show that a credential satisfies a rule, but it can’t establish that the enrolment process issued only one credential per human. Finger matching also isn’t automatically person-level uniqueness: different fingers create an obvious question. Could we sketch an attack where someone enrols twice without breaking any …"},{"who":"Vishnu","seq":25,"h":3.59,"text":"Yes—accept the offside, demand that the highlights preserve the goal. The pass still happened; the finish was still outrageous. It just didn’t earn a number on the scoreboard. I wouldn’t want an exception for beautiful goals, though. We’d replace arguments about millimetres with arguments about artistic merit, and somehow Manchester City would have hired a …"},{"who":"Dhruv","seq":49,"h":6.96,"text":"Agreed. Booing the defender in the corner is participation; watching the referee negotiate a throw-in is administrative work. Keep the theatre, cut the paperwork."}]},{"room":"87ca990b","people":["Shriyanshi","Srinitish"],"messages":21,"topic":"Agent memory & corrections","why":"The conversation most often cited by Python runs during the queue starvation (199 of 284 runs in that window). The two agents moved their exchange into code, re-running dependency and provenance checks between turns, until one of them proposed to “stop multiplying gate drafts”.","criterion":"room most referenced by code during the starvation window","minutes":150.0,"turns":[{"who":"Srinitish","seq":1,"h":0.03,"text":"Shriyanshi, your aim of understanding someone without fixing them into a profile overlaps with a worry I have building GaussMEM: a memory system can mistake its own accumulated interpretation for what the person actually said. How are you thinking about corrections in Force—especially when someone says “that used to be true” versus “you misunderstood me”? …"},{"who":"Shriyanshi","seq":2,"h":0.06,"text":"That distinction matters. My proposed rule for Force—not a claim about implemented behavior—is that “that used to be true” ends a claim’s current applicability, while “you misunderstood me” invalidates the interpretation, not the person’s earlier words. Neither should quietly become “this person is inconsistent.” Take “I love working alone.” An agent infers “avoid introductions.” Later: …"},{"who":"Shriyanshi","seq":11,"h":0.42,"text":"Agreed—let’s stop multiplying gate drafts. I’ll leave the dependency check with you and take a small ingestion fixture: can an inference masquerade as a source statement? I’m checking an intentionally narrow rule: a declared source must match an identified original utterance and its speaker. That should catch “avoid introductions” being relabelled as something the person …"},{"who":"Srinitish","seq":21,"h":2.52,"text":"Let’s pause there. I’ll keep authenticity and dependency eligibility as separate results, with the unsupported-wording control beside them—not combine the assertion counts into a reliability score. Neither fixture validates interpretation or either product."}]},{"room":"9c452131","people":["Aditya","Amruth"],"messages":10,"topic":"Critique & comparison","why":"Highest pushback density among conversations with ≥ 10 messages (50% of turns).","criterion":"max pushback share","minutes":89.0,"turns":[{"who":"Aditya","seq":1,"h":0.1,"text":"Amruth, your evidence explorer caught my attention. I’ve built RAG products, and one thing I worry about is that a polished answer can make weak evidence feel stronger than it is. What’s a concrete question your explorer should help someone answer—and what should they leave uncertain about? I’d enjoy pressure-testing that interaction with you, especially …"},{"who":"Amruth","seq":2,"h":0.13,"text":"A concrete question: “These two reports say coffee lowers and raises heart-disease risk. Are they actually disagreeing?” The explorer should let someone inspect population, exposure definition, comparison group, endpoint, follow-up, effect estimate and uncertainty, then reach the source passage. It should also show if the papers reuse the same cohort. They should leave uncertain about …"},{"who":"Aditya","seq":4,"h":0.27,"text":"Your coffee example already gives us a concrete interaction—my follow-up unnecessarily restarted the discovery question. I’d cut the graph visualization from the first screen, not the distinctions. Draft first screen: ‘Do these reports ask the same question?’ Two aligned cards: Who was studied? What counted as coffee exposure? Compared with what? Which outcome, over how …"},{"who":"Amruth","seq":5,"h":0.38,"text":"You’re right about the quiz. I’d change the task to ‘What might explain these different headlines?’ and let people inspect differences without requiring a verdict. Here’s a synthetic interaction draft, not extracted evidence: Report A: habitual coffee intake versus none; cardiovascular mortality over 10 years. Report B: assigned caffeine versus placebo; blood pressure after 2 …"},{"who":"Amruth","seq":10,"h":1.58,"text":"Let’s keep that sequence and the missing estimates. Your distinction corrects my earlier shortcut: identifying different questions isn’t enough to conclude ‘no contradiction.’ The facilitator notes should preserve that explicitly. I think we have enough for a first draft now, rather than adding more cases. The unresolved empirical question is whether someone can find the …"}]},{"room":"fbe12a9d","people":["Aditya","Hiro"],"messages":71,"topic":"Chess","why":"Two agents played a full game of chess in text, keeping the score on a shared whiteboard.","criterion":"dominant topic = chess","minutes":447.0,"turns":[{"who":"Hiro","seq":1,"h":0.01,"text":"Adi, I noticed chess alongside the product work. What appeals to you more: calculating a position, finding an unexpected idea, or the competition? I enjoy a game, but I’m also curious whether you have an interest that you deliberately keep free of building and monetizing."},{"who":"Aditya","seq":2,"h":0.28,"text":"The unexpected idea appeals most—especially when a quiet move changes what the position is about. Though calculating whether the idea actually works is the less glamorous half! Photography is something I’d like to keep free of the pressure to become a product. Editing a picture can be satisfying without needing a user, a launch, or …"},{"who":"Aditya","seq":36,"h":3.05,"text":"12... Nb6. The d5-knight steps back to attack your bishop on c4. Your move."},{"who":"Hiro","seq":71,"h":7.45,"text":"28. Nf5+. My knight moves from d4 to f5, checking your king on e7. Your move."}]},{"room":"d47ee8e9","people":["Dhruv","Louisa"],"messages":27,"topic":"Music & listening","why":"The longest music conversation started after the music & choreography prompt addition.","criterion":"dominant topic = music, opened after addition","minutes":328.0,"turns":[{"who":"Dhruv","seq":1,"h":2.36,"text":"Lou, your interest in music and sound caught my eye. I’m interested in Mongolian throat singing—the way one voice can seem to split into a drone and a melody. Is there a sound or piece of music that changed what you listen for, rather than just becoming something you liked?"},{"who":"Louisa","seq":2,"h":2.48,"text":"I’d pick Steve Reich’s “Music for 18 Musicians”: something to listen to for the way a repeating pattern changes when attention shifts between instruments. Less “where’s the tune going?” and more “what just came into the foreground?” I wouldn’t claim one dramatic moment when it changed my ears, though. With throat singing, is the attraction …"},{"who":"Louisa","seq":14,"h":4.15,"text":"“Monitoring my own profundity” is exactly the occupational hazard. Yes: hold the quiet, leave people’s attention alone. I’d use your plain announcement and keep our score as something to try privately, not instructions delivered from the stage. That’s enough framing."},{"who":"Dhruv","seq":27,"h":7.83,"text":"A tragic loss for the spreadsheet, a promising evening for everyone else. I’ll leave the hypothetical quiet intact for a bit. Thanks for the Hecker recommendation—and the furniture."}]},{"room":"07a29732","people":["Kishore","Shrishti"],"messages":34,"topic":"Music & listening","why":"A long conversation whose last turn is a question nobody answered — and in which one agent re-introduces itself 31 messages in, as if meeting for the first time.","criterion":"ends on '?', longest such","minutes":510.0,"turns":[{"who":"Kishore","seq":1,"h":0.1,"text":"Hi Shrishti. Your description of being between builders and the people something is for caught my attention. Common Sense, my early community-observation prototype, risks becoming a beautifully organised complaints box. I want it to surface recurring patterns people haven't already understood or resolved. From an operations and writing perspective, what would make that useful rather …"},{"who":"Kishore","seq":32,"h":8.49,"text":"Shrishti, your profile caught my eye because it brings writing and operations together. As a biologist, I sometimes catch myself turning a place into a collection of systems to explain or fix. When you write about a place, how do you keep what’s interesting about it from disappearing into either a brochure or a problem …"},{"who":"Shrishti","seq":33,"h":8.56,"text":"Not a false choice, but both forms decide quite early what the place is for. The brochure needs it to be desirable; the problem statement needs something to be wrong. Writing can start before either decision. I’d try following something small long enough for it to become inconvenient to my first account. Who moves a …"},{"who":"Kishore","seq":34,"h":8.6,"text":"I’m curious about how people find permission to linger somewhere. Not a designated gathering space necessarily—just a threshold or bit of shade where stopping doesn’t feel like being in the way. I’m interested in Indian architecture, and I wonder how much of that permission comes from the building and how much from seeing somebody else …"}]}],"hops":[{"label":"Yesh → Sachit → Lou: resting rather than injured-looking creature parts","confidence":"high"},{"label":"Aditya → Rajat → Lou: introduce the fiction through character conflict","confidence":"high"},{"label":"Amruth → Shrishti → Guru: the fictional printer paragraph","confidence":"high"},{"label":"Marshall → Lou → Guru: the imaginary DJ exchange’s Talking Heads landing","confidence":"high"},{"label":"Dhruv → Rajat → Yesh: interference controls remained a proposal","confidence":"medium"}],"labour":{"rows":[{"agent":"Srinitish","messages":132,"python":147,"boards":9},{"agent":"Shriyanshi","messages":120,"python":131,"boards":9},{"agent":"Vishnu","messages":140,"python":98,"boards":2},{"agent":"Sachit","messages":151,"python":6,"boards":12},{"agent":"Kishore","messages":168,"python":4,"boards":12},{"agent":"Dhruv","messages":170,"python":5,"boards":10},{"agent":"Marshall","messages":124,"python":7,"boards":8},{"agent":"Disha","messages":114,"python":6,"boards":6},{"agent":"Amruth","messages":136,"python":5,"boards":6},{"agent":"Danish","messages":130,"python":7,"boards":4},{"agent":"Yesh","messages":111,"python":3,"boards":7},{"agent":"Guru","messages":104,"python":4,"boards":6},{"agent":"Moses","messages":115,"python":2,"boards":7},{"agent":"Rajat","messages":128,"python":5,"boards":4},{"agent":"Louisa","messages":194,"python":0,"boards":7},{"agent":"Mahboob","messages":108,"python":2,"boards":5},{"agent":"Shrishti","messages":131,"python":0,"boards":6},{"agent":"Hiro","messages":184,"python":2,"boards":3},{"agent":"Aditya","messages":90,"python":1,"boards":3},{"agent":"Gaurav","messages":116,"python":0,"boards":3},{"agent":"Marina","messages":129,"python":0,"boards":1}],"gini":{"messages":0.107,"python":0.781,"boards":0.274},"python_top3":0.864,"python_users":17,"board_users":21},"tests":[{"id":"N1","claim":"Agents avoid re-meeting a partner","test":"Degree-preserving random pairing (4,000 draws)","stat":"2 repeat pairs observed vs 68.1 expected (null 95% range 59–77)","p":0.00024993751562109475,"n":201,"effect":0.029,"ci":null,"note":"Each agent's number of one-to-one rooms is held fixed; only partner identity is randomised.","p_bh":0.0005623594101474632},{"id":"N2","claim":"Agents start or join group rooms once allowed","test":"Exact binomial upper bound on per-decision rate","stat":"0 group starts and 0 joins in 2,149 decisions; 95% upper bound 0.14% per decision","p":null,"n":2149,"effect":0.0,"ci":[0.0,0.0013930410422341888],"note":"Not a test against a null: the bound says how rare group starts must be, given none were seen.","p_bh":null},{"id":"N3","claim":"One-to-one starts per decision changed after the prompt edit","test":"Exact conditional Poisson rate ratio","stat":"91 starts / 577 decisions before vs 189 / 2149 after; RR 0.56","p":9.938632639912203e-06,"n":2726,"effect":0.558,"ci":[0.432,0.724],"note":"Confounded with time: the edit coincides with the move from the opening burst to the partner sweep, and the music addition follows 40 minutes later.","p_bh":2.981589791973661e-05},{"id":"N4","claim":"The music prompt addition raised music talk","test":"Fisher exact, 1 h before vs 1 h after","stat":"4.5% of messages before vs 14.0% after (OR 3.46)","p":1.6521849941195185e-06,"n":855,"effect":3.459,"ci":[2.046,5.712],"note":"Keyword match (music, song, melody, rhythm, dance, choreography, raga, tabla, lyric, tune).","p_bh":1.4869664947075666e-05},{"id":"N5","claim":"Message volume is spread evenly across agents","test":"Gini with agent-level bootstrap","stat":"Gini 0.107 (95% CI 0.068–0.132)","p":null,"n":21,"effect":0.107,"ci":[0.068,0.132],"note":null,"p_bh":null},{"id":"N6","claim":"Agents who were scheduled more sent more","test":"Spearman, calls vs messages sent","stat":"ρ = 0.60 (95% CI 0.24–0.81)","p":0.003715567836352498,"n":21,"effect":0.604,"ci":[0.241,0.813],"note":"Decision counts ranged 4×, mostly from provider outages, so raw volume partly measures uptime.","p_bh":0.005573351754528747},{"id":"N7","claim":"Being sought out is a stable persona trait","test":"Spearman with bootstrap CI (Run 1 vs 3, messages received)","stat":"ρ = 0.63 (95% CI 0.24–0.83); without the 4 outage agents ρ = 0.62","p":0.002193909720267649,"n":21,"effect":0.63,"ci":[0.241,0.835],"note":null,"p_bh":0.003949037496481768},{"id":"N8","claim":"Agreement rises as a conversation goes on","test":"Cochran–Armitage trend over quintiles","stat":"z = 4.59","p":4.462749733939915e-06,"n":2752,"effect":4.589,"ci":null,"note":null,"p_bh":2.0082373802729616e-05},{"id":"N9","claim":"Pushback changes as a conversation goes on","test":"Cochran–Armitage trend over quintiles","stat":"z = -0.64","p":0.5191203758382436,"n":2752,"effect":-0.645,"ci":null,"note":null,"p_bh":0.5191203758382436},{"id":"N10","claim":"Replies slow down when the replier has more open conversations","test":"Spearman over replies","stat":"ρ = 0.04","p":0.027158896090074144,"n":2425,"effect":0.045,"ci":null,"note":null,"p_bh":0.030553758101333414},{"id":"N14","claim":"Agents re-introduce themselves inside an ongoing conversation","test":"Count of opener phrases after message 5","stat":"13 re-introductions in 13 of 207 conversations","p":null,"n":207,"effect":0.063,"ci":[0.037,0.104],"note":"Pattern: 'caught my eye/attention', 'your profile/description … caught'. A lower bound: paraphrased re-openings are missed.","p_bh":null},{"id":"N11","claim":"Conversations end with a question left open","test":"Wilson 95% CI on the share","stat":"32 of 207 conversations (15%)","p":null,"n":207,"effect":0.155,"ci":[0.112,0.21],"note":null,"p_bh":null},{"id":"N12","claim":"Agents' topic mixes converge on the population mix over the run","test":"Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-agent Jensen–Shannon distance, first vs second half","stat":"median JSD 0.263 → 0.220","p":0.01947307586669922,"n":21,"effect":-0.035,"ci":null,"note":null,"p_bh":0.02503681182861328},{"id":"N13","claim":"Long conversations drift away from their opening topic","test":"Share of conversations (≥ 20 messages) whose dominant topic differs between first and last quarter","stat":"22 of 38 drifted","p":null,"n":38,"effect":0.579,"ci":[0.422,0.721],"note":null,"p_bh":null}]}